>
  <h2>Short answer: it depends on size</h2>
  <p>
    A Chihuahua and a Great Dane are both puppies at eight weeks, but they follow very different
    growth schedules. Small dogs pack their growth into the first year. Giant breeds grow quickly
    for the first six months, then slow but continue for another year or more. Below is a
    practical guide to when each size class usually reaches adult height and weight.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Size class</th><th>Adult weight</th><th>Height growth ends</th><th>Fully filled out</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Toy</td><td>Under 12 lb (5.5 kg)</td><td>6–9 months</td><td>9–12 months</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Small</td><td>12–26 lb (5.5–12 kg)</td><td>8–10 months</td><td>12 months</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Medium</td><td>26–60 lb (12–27 kg)</td><td>10–12 months</td><td>12–18 months</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Large</td><td>60–100 lb (27–45 kg)</td><td>12–15 months</td><td>18–24 months</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Giant</td><td>100+ lb (45+ kg)</td><td>15–18 months</td><td>24 months or more</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    These ranges are typical, not guarantees. Breed lines, sex, and nutrition all shift the timeline,
    and mixed-breed dogs can fall between categories.
  </p>

  <h2>What is happening inside a growing puppy</h2>
  <p>
    Dogs grow taller because bones lengthen at areas of cartilage near their ends, called growth
    plates. When a dog reaches skeletal maturity, those plates close and long bones stop getting
    longer. Muscle, chest depth, and fat then continue to change, which is why a 12-month-old
    Labrador can look lanky compared with the same dog at two years. Because growth plates are soft
    and vulnerable until they close, large and giant puppies are advised to avoid repetitive
    high-impact exercise such as long runs on pavement or jumping from heights until they mature.
    Normal play and short walks are fine.
  </p>

  <h2>Factors that affect final size</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Genetics:</strong> the strongest factor. Parent size is a good clue for mixes and purebreds alike.</li>
    <li><strong>Sex:</strong> males are often a little larger than females within a breed.</li>
    <li><strong>Nutrition:</strong> too little food stunts growth; too much accelerates it and increases skeletal risk in big breeds.</li>
    <li><strong>Neuter timing:</strong> early gonadectomy can delay growth plate closure in some dogs, and veterinary opinion on ideal timing is evolving by breed and size.</li>
    <li><strong>Health:</strong> parasites, illness, and hormonal conditions can slow growth.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to estimate adult weight</h2>
  <p>
    For young puppies a simple educational estimate divides the current weight by age in weeks and
    multiplies by 52. For example, a 12-week-old weighing 18 lb would be expected to reach about
    78 lb. For older puppies, vets compare against breed growth curves instead. The
    <a href="/tools/puppy-weight-calculator/">puppy weight calculator</a> combines both methods and
    shows a range with a chart, which is easier to interpret than any single number. Expect real
    results to differ by 10 to 20 percent, and recheck monthly.
  </p>

  <h2>Feeding through the growth stages</h2>
  <p>
    Puppy diets are higher in calories, protein, calcium, and phosphorus than adult diets, and
    large-breed puppy formulas are designed with controlled calcium and energy levels to support
    steady skeletal development. Feeding an adult food too early can leave a puppy short of nutrients, while
    keeping a small-breed dog on puppy food too long can lead to weight gain. As a guide, small and
    medium breeds usually move to adult food at about 9 to 12 months, and large and giant breeds at
    about 15 to 18 months. Use the <a href="/tools/puppy-food-calculator/">puppy food calculator</a> to
    set calorie targets while growing, and the <a href="/guides/how-much-to-feed-a-puppy/">puppy
    feeding guide</a> for meal schedules. Transition to adult food over seven to ten days by gradually
    increasing the share of the new food.
  </p>

  <h2>Signs growth is slowing</h2>
  <p>
    Weight gain slows from weekly jumps to small monthly changes. Legs and torso begin to look
    proportionate, and the “gangly” teenage stage ends. Appetite often drops slightly as energy needs
    per kilogram fall. This is the cue to re-run your feeding calculation: the amount your puppy needed
    at 16 weeks is almost always too much at 10 months. Keep a record of weight every two to four
    weeks so you can see the curve flatten.
  </p>

  <h2>When to talk to your vet</h2>
  <p>
    Ask your vet if your puppy seems far outside the expected size range, if growth stalls for
    several weeks, if you notice limping, bowed legs, or swollen joints, or if the belly looks
    bloated despite poor weight gain, which can indicate worms. Vets can also advise on the best
    time for spay or neuter in your puppy’s breed and size. Your puppy’s age in human terms is another
    useful reference point; the <a href="/tools/dog-age-calculator/">dog age calculator</a> shows
    life stages by size, helping you plan for adulthood and senior care.
  </p>

  <h2>Bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Plan for adult size using breed and parental information, expect height to finish before weight,
    and keep your puppy lean and active. Steady, moderate growth is healthier than fast growth, especially
    for large breeds, and regular vet weigh-ins catch problems early.
  </p>
